# Angular RTL Layout & Universal Tokenisation Rules

When developing UI components in Angular v22:

1. **Universal Word Tokenisation (`Intl.Segmenter`)**
   - Never use `string.split(' ')` or regular expressions to segment words for clickable tokens or dictionary popups.
   - Always use the native browser `Intl.Segmenter` API (`granularity: 'word'`):
     ```typescript
     const segmenter = new Intl.Segmenter(locale, { granularity: 'word' });
     const tokens = Array.from(segmenter.segment(text));
     ```

2. **Logical Tailwind Properties for RTL Mirroring**
   - The UI natively mirrors for Arabic, Hebrew, and Persian.
   - Never use physical direction utilities (`pl-4`, `pr-2`, `ml-3`, `mr-1`, `border-l`, `border-r`, `text-left`, `text-right`).
   - Strictly use Tailwind logical properties (`ps-4`, `pe-2`, `ms-3`, `me-1`, `border-s`, `border-e`, `text-start`, `text-end`).

3. **Zero Hard-Coded Strings & Universal Translation System (`TranslatePipe` / `I18nService`)**
   - Never write raw hard-coded user-facing strings inside Angular templates (`*.html`) or component logic (`*.ts`).
   - In templates, always import and use `TranslatePipe` from `src/app/services/translate.pipe.ts`:
     ```html
     <span>{{ 'common.submit' | t }}</span>
     <p>{{ 'welcome.message' | t: { name: userName() } }}</p>
     ```
   - In programmatic services or TypeScript logic, inject and call `I18nService` from `src/app/services/i18n.service.ts`:
     ```typescript
     const i18n = inject(I18nService);
     const title = i18n.translate('app.title');
     ```
   - The `I18nService` dynamically checks cached dictionaries, fetches missing translations from the backend (`/nlp/translate-ui`), and automatically manages RTL/LTR document directionality.
